# Getting started with SmolBox

```elixir
Mix.install([{:smolbox, "~> 0.4.2"}])
```

## Prepare the worker

This notebook runs Python, collects `42` from an output file and waits for VM
cleanup. Run Livebook locally on the worker host. The Linux recipe requires
x86_64 with working KVM, smolvm 1.22.0 and enough space for the runtime image and
20/10 GiB disk allocations.

For macOS Apple Silicon, log into a dedicated account with no existing smolvm
machines. Install smolvm and run image preparation, the worker and Livebook in
that same account. The demo directory is private; a Livebook session running
under another account cannot read it. Launch a new Livebook session under the
dedicated account, then import this notebook there. Follow the
[macOS worker setup](https://hexdocs.pm/smolbox/getting-started.html#macos-worker-setup)
instead of the Linux shell commands below; `SMOLVM_DATA_DIR` does not isolate
worker state on the pinned macOS build.

If `smolvm --version` does not already report `1.22.0`, run the
[upstream installer from the 1.22.0 release](https://github.com/smol-machines/smolvm/blob/v1.22.0/scripts/install.sh)
in a Bash terminal as your normal user, with `curl` and `tar` available:

```bash
SMOLBOX_INSTALLER="$(mktemp)"
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/smol-machines/smolvm/v1.22.0/scripts/install.sh \
  -o "$SMOLBOX_INSTALLER" &&
  bash "$SMOLBOX_INSTALLER" --version 1.22.0
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
smolvm --version
```

Continue only when the version reports `1.22.0`. This installs the matching
distribution, including libraries, agent and disk templates. The Elixir dependency
does not install a worker. The installer replaces this account's smolvm installation;
use a separate account to preserve another installed version.

On Linux, check KVM access before preparing the image:

```bash
test -r /dev/kvm && test -w /dev/kvm && echo "KVM access ready"
```

Continue only when this prints `KVM access ready`. Otherwise, resolve KVM
availability and permissions on the worker host first.

The guest runs offline. SmolBox rejects inventory entries with networking enabled
without explicit allowlists, including machines created outside SmolBox. Give
this demo its own worker state and endpoint, keeping your existing machines intact.

Run this in a Bash terminal on the worker host (inside your Nix shell if used):

```bash
export SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT="$(mktemp -d)"
chmod 700 "$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT"
mkdir -m 700 "$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT/images" "$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT/worker" "$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT/objects"
cd "$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT/images"
export SMOLVM_DATA_DIR="$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT/worker"

smolvm pack create --image python:3.12-alpine --entrypoint /bin/true \
  --cpus 1 --mem 256 --staging-dir ./staging --output ./python

printf 'Demo directory: %s\n' "$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT"
sha256sum "$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT/images/python.smolmachine"
```

Record the directory and SHA256 printed above. In the same terminal, start the
worker and leave it running:

```bash
env SMOLVM_DATA_DIR="$SMOLBOX_DEMO_ROOT/worker" \
  SMOLVM_GUEST_ROLLOUT_HOST_PORT=19472 \
  SMOLVM_FILE_TRANSFER_MAX_BYTES=1048576 \
  smolvm serve start --listen 127.0.0.1:19471
```

In a second terminal, check that this returns `{"machines":[]}`:

```sh
curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:19471/api/v1/machines
```

If either port is occupied, choose unused HTTP and guest rollout ports; update
`runtime_url` below to match the HTTP port. Preparation downloads the Python
image, but execution remains offline. For controlled network access later, see
[Network access](https://hexdocs.pm/smolbox/network-access.html).

## Configure the demo

Replace the directory and SHA256 with the values from preparation. The digest is
operator approval of the image you just prepared; the next cell checks it before
using the artifact.

```elixir
demo_root = "/absolute/path/printed/by/setup"
approved_sha256 = "paste_the_sha256_printed_by_setup"
runtime_url = "http://127.0.0.1:19471"
```

## Connect to the empty worker

Verify the image, worker version and inventory, then start the supervised runtime.
If this cell fails, check the worker terminal and configuration before continuing.

```elixir
alias SmolBox.{Command, ExecutionSpec, Files, Profile, Worker}
alias SmolBox.ArtifactStore.Directory
alias SmolBox.Runtime.WorkerConfig
alias SmolBox.Store.Memory

artifact_path = Path.join(demo_root, "images/python.smolmachine")

actual_sha256 =
  artifact_path
  |> File.stream!(65_536)
  |> Enum.reduce(:crypto.hash_init(:sha256), &:crypto.hash_update(&2, &1))
  |> :crypto.hash_final()
  |> Base.encode16(case: :lower)

true = actual_sha256 == approved_sha256

{platform, architecture} =
  case :os.type() do
    {:unix, :darwin} -> {:macos, "aarch64"}
    {:unix, :linux} -> {:linux, "x86_64"}
  end

worker_options = [
  allow_insecure_loopback: true,
  operation_timeout_ms: 60_000,
  receive_timeout_ms: 55_000
]

{:ok, worker} =
  Worker.new("demo-worker", runtime_url, worker_options)

{:ok, client} = SmolBox.Client.new(worker)
runtime_version = "1.22.0"
{:ok, %{version: ^runtime_version}} = SmolBox.Client.health(client)
:ok = SmolBox.Client.readiness(client)
case SmolBox.Client.list(client) do
  {:ok, []} ->
    :ok

  {:ok, _machines} ->
    raise "This demo needs an empty worker. Use the separate worker from the preparation section."

  {:error, error} ->
    raise "Could not validate the demo worker inventory: #{inspect(error)}. " <>
            "Check the endpoint and use the separate worker from the preparation section."
end

{:ok, profile} =
  Profile.new("demo-offline-v1", storage_gb: 20, overlay_gb: 10, host_overhead_mb: 768)

artifact = %{
  "id" => "demo-python-v1",
  "sha256" => approved_sha256,
  "architecture" => architecture,
  "path" => artifact_path
}

{:ok, configured_worker} =
  WorkerConfig.new(
    client: client,
    platform: platform,
    architecture: architecture,
    runtime_version: runtime_version,
    artifacts: [artifact],
    profiles: [profile],
    allocation_floor: %{storage_gb: 20, overlay_gb: 10, host_overhead_mb: 768},
    capacity: %{slots: 1, cpus: 1, memory_mb: 1024, disk_gb: 30}
  )

{:ok, objects} = Directory.new(Path.join(demo_root, "objects"))

{:ok, supervisor} =
  Supervisor.start_link(
    [
      {Memory, name: SmolBoxDemo.Store},
      {SmolBox,
       name: SmolBoxDemo.Runtime,
       namespace: "sbxdemo",
       mode: :ephemeral,
       store: {Memory, SmolBoxDemo.Store},
       artifact_store: {Directory, objects},
       fingerprint_key: :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32),
       workers: [configured_worker],
       max_active: 1}
    ],
    strategy: :rest_for_one
  )
```

## Run Python and collect the answer

The second submission uses the same identity and returns the same handle. The
cell checks that the output file contains `42` and that Python exited successfully.

```elixir
scope = "demo"
id = "hello-" <> Base.encode16(:crypto.strong_rand_bytes(6), case: :lower)
source_ref = "source-" <> id

source = """
from pathlib import Path
Path('/workspace/result.txt').write_text('42\\n')
print('hello from SmolBox')
"""

:ok = Directory.seed(objects, scope, source_ref, source)

{:ok, command} = Command.new(["python", "/workspace/main.py"], timeout_secs: 10)

{:ok, spec} =
  ExecutionSpec.new(
    scope: scope,
    id: id,
    artifact: Map.drop(artifact, ["path"]),
    profile: profile,
    command: command,
    inputs: [
      %{
        "source" => source_ref,
        "path" => "/workspace/main.py",
        "size" => byte_size(source),
        "sha256" => Files.sha256(source),
        "mode" => "runtime_default"
      }
    ],
    outputs: [
      %{
        "destination" => "answer",
        "path" => "/workspace/result.txt",
        "max_bytes" => 1024
      }
    ]
  )

{:ok, handle} = SmolBox.submit(SmolBoxDemo.Runtime, spec)
{:ok, ^handle} = SmolBox.submit(SmolBoxDemo.Runtime, spec)
{:ok, record} = SmolBox.await(SmolBoxDemo.Runtime, handle, 90_000)
%{state: :completed, result: %{exit_code: 0}, collection: :complete} = record
IO.write(record.result.stdout)
{:ok, "42\n"} = Directory.read_output(objects, handle, "answer", 1024)
```

## Wait for cleanup

A completed command can still have cleanup pending. This cell waits for deletion
and capacity release before stopping the demo supervisor.

```elixir
# await/3 returns the command/collection outcome before cleanup may finish.
wait_for_cleanup = fn again, deadline ->
  {:ok, current} = SmolBox.fetch(SmolBoxDemo.Runtime, scope, id)

  cond do
    current.cleanup == :complete and current.reservation == nil ->
      current

    System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) >= deadline ->
      raise "Cleanup is still pending. Keep IEx open and inspect the runtime."

    true ->
      Process.sleep(100)
      again.(again, deadline)
  end
end

cleaned =
  wait_for_cleanup.(wait_for_cleanup, System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + 60_000)

IO.inspect(Map.take(cleaned, [:state, :collection, :cleanup, :reservation]),
  label: "finished"
)

:ok = Supervisor.stop(supervisor)
```

Expected output: `hello from SmolBox`, then a summary with `state: :completed`,
`collection: :complete`, `cleanup: :complete` and `reservation: nil`.

When finished, stop the worker with Ctrl+C. The execution store is in memory;
its records disappear when the supervisor stops. Your demo directory retains the
Python image and input/output files. Keep it for inspection, then remove only
that directory when no longer needed.

For restart recovery and application integration, continue with
[Managed host integration](https://hexdocs.pm/smolbox/host-integration.html).
